/* ODISSEI Annual Plan 2027: all text and figures for the page.
   Edit this file to change content; annual-plan-2027.html only handles layout.
   Text comes from the Google Doc "Content - Annual plan 2027".
   - Values in [square brackets] are shown as "still to be set" until replaced.
   - In plans: t = heading, s = short summary for the card,
     full = the complete text from the plan. Items marked key:true are shown
     as cards (three per tab); every item appears in the full list under the
     section's "Read all" button.
   - Write text between backticks (`like this`). */

/* ---------- Workstreams ---------- */
const WS=[
 {id:"data",name:"Data Facility",color:"var(--ws-data)",img:"img/odissei-keynote.jpg",alt:"Keynote speaker on stage at the ODISSEI Conference",credit:"Photo: nelinadasha.com for ODISSEI",
  head:`From finding data to analysing it <span class="hl">safely, in one route</span>`,
  aim:"Find, request and analyse sensitive data in one route",
  lead:`Some of the most valuable social science data are also the most sensitive. The Data Facility makes it possible for researchers to find such data, request access, and analyse it in a protected setting where the data owner stays in control.`,
  facts:[["20 → 67","SANE research projects, against a target of 30"],["~2 weeks","Target time to access, down from three to four"]],
  highlights:["Access in about two weeks instead of three to four","OSSC opened to a first provider beyond CBS","FIRMBACKBONE from request to workspace by spring"],
  intro:[
   `Some of the most valuable social science data are also the most sensitive: tax and income records, health information, and data about people’s online behaviour. The Data Facility makes it possible for researchers to find such data, request access, and analyse it in a protected setting where the data owner stays in control.`,
   `The facility has several parts that work together. CBS provides access to its rich microdata. SANE is a secure online workspace where researchers can analyse data from many different providers. The OSSC offers protected computing power for complex analyses of CBS microdata. The ODISSEI Portal and the DANS archive help researchers discover datasets and ensure results are preserved for future use. A new tool, the Data Access Broker (DAB), handles access requests digitally, much like an online application form that knows each provider’s rules. A Knowledge Graph (KG) supports this by connecting datasets, variables, access conditions, code, software, and publications, making it faster to find what researchers need and easier to reproduce research. In addition, ODISSEI supports data reuse through its FAIR Expertise Hub, which advises researchers and data providers on FAIR data practices.`,
   `2026 was a year of strong growth. The number of research projects using SANE more than tripled, from 20 to 67, far above the target of 30. Logging in became much easier, and the first data provider started handling requests through the Data Access Broker. In 2027, the aim is to join these pieces into one smooth route: a researcher finds a dataset, requests it, and receives a ready-to-use secure workspace, with access granted in about two weeks instead of the current three to four. The main challenge is to keep up with demand while building these new features.`],
  status:{big:"20 → 67",cap:"research projects using SANE in 2026, against a target of 30",text:[
   `In 2026, microdata usage remained high, and facilities for finding and requesting data were further developed. At CBS, uploading large external datasets to the OSSC became simpler, and tools are being developed to make complex data easier to use and output checking more efficient. CBS is also piloting synthetic “practice” versions of some datasets so researchers can prepare before using the real data. To support reuse and reproducibility, the FAIR Expertise Hub published a FAIR action plan, organised replication games with CBS data, and supported data providers and research projects.`,
   `The ODISSEI Portal became more stable, and a development plan was drafted. The Knowledge Graph now holds ODISSEI’s standard access conditions (based on the Five Safes Framework) in machine-readable form, and the new SSH FAIR Vocabulary Registry helps researchers find shared vocabularies. The DAB is moving from proof of concept to a first stable release, with FIRMBACKBONE as the first provider to test a complete request.`,
   `SANE became a service that researchers can use without specialist help. Use grew well beyond its target, including outside the social sciences, and researchers can now access their environment in a web browser. ODISSEI brokers between researchers, data providers and SURF, and in September 2026 introduced a fixed credit budget per project to keep costs predictable.`]},
  plans:[
   {t:"Easier-to-find and easier-to-use CBS data",s:"Persistent identifiers, a published data catalogue, English documentation and faster output checking.",full:`CBS will issue its own persistent identifiers and publish its data catalogue, and will actively raise awareness of its facilities through events and communication with its users. It will start translating documentation into English, and introduce an application that checks research outputs faster and more transparently.`},
   {key:true,t:"Secure computing beyond CBS",s:"The OSSC is upgraded and opened to a first provider other than CBS; a federated SANE prototype.",full:`The OSSC will be upgraded and opened to a first data provider other than CBS. A prototype of a “federated” SANE, which analyses data where they are stored instead of moving them, will link SURF, the Netherlands Institute for Sound & Vision and the KB.`},
   {t:"More and better features",s:"Quarterly SANE releases based on what researchers in current projects need.",when:"Each quarter",full:`SANE will release improvements every quarter based on what researchers in current projects need, such as easier use of local AI language models, reopening closed projects to reproduce results, and better support for projects that combine data from several providers.`},
   {key:true,t:"One secure route from search to analysis",s:"An approved request automatically creates a SANE workspace. FIRMBACKBONE goes first.",when:"Spring",full:`An approved request should automatically create a secure workspace in SANE and deliver the data, without manual steps. FIRMBACKBONE, a dataset on Dutch companies, goes first (by spring), followed by the KB’s web archive collection.`},
   {key:true,t:"A sustainable service",s:"The SANE credit model is evaluated in June, and standard agreements speed up onboarding.",when:"June",full:`The credit model for using SANE will be evaluated in June 2027, and the boards will receive a recommendation. Standard agreements for data providers will make onboarding quicker, and ODISSEI and SURF will agree by spring how international researchers can use SANE.`},
   {t:"A richer Portal and archive",s:"More member organisations covered, better search, and requesting several datasets at once.",full:`The Portal will extend its coverage to member organisations whose data are not yet included, improve search results, and connect more closely to the DAB so researchers can select and request multiple datasets at once.`},
   {t:"Knowledge Graph development",s:"A roadmap, first prototypes, and a pilot that makes studies reproducible in a few steps.",full:`It will result in a roadmap and first prototypes. A pilot will connect the Knowledge Graph to the DAB and SANE, with records of data references, code, software, and access conditions for a study, so it can be reproduced in a secure environment in a few steps.`},
   {t:"Consolidation in FAIR Expertise Hub",s:"Onboarding more data providers to the secure workflow, publishing vocabularies, workshops and blogs.",full:`FAIR steward will help onboard more data providers to the secure data workflow of Portal, DAB and SANE, publish vocabularies that make data more FAIR, and continue its dissemination work through workshops and blogs.`},
   {t:"European opportunities",s:"Two pending European proposals for SANE could add capacity and new users.",full:`The outcomes of two European proposals for SANE, TRELISS and FIERSE, are still pending; if funded, they would add development capacity and new users.`}],
  items:[["CBS microdata support",478696],["Portal coverage and interoperability",1083488],["Secure computing on the OSSC",523337],["SANE, incl. FIRMBACKBONE",966661]],
  budgetText:`Planned 2027 expenditure is €3,052,182. This includes €478,696 allocated to CBS for continuous support in using the CBS microdata, while efforts to ensure interoperability and expand Portal coverage are budgeted at a total of €1,083,488. A total of €523,337 is allocated to secure computing via OSSC. In addition, €966,661 will be directed to improvements in SANE, both in terms of technical development and making Firmbackbone infrastructure accessible in this environment.`},

 {id:"observatory",name:"Observatory",color:"var(--ws-obs)",img:"img/observatory.jpg",alt:"A survey participant holds a tablet",credit:"Photo: ODISSEI",
  head:`Good research starts with <span class="hl">good data</span>`,
  aim:"Long-running collections and new kinds of data",
  lead:`The Observatory sustains the long-running data collections the community relies on and opens up kinds of data that did not exist a few years ago.`,
  facts:[["7,500","People in 5,000 households in the LISS panel"],["14,000+","Registered users of the LISS Data Archive"]],
  highlights:["PANL opens its public beta","First wave of the Web & Media Diets Observatory","First shared question bank for international surveys"],
  intro:[
   `Good research starts with good data, and ODISSEI delivers it. The workstream sustains the long-running data collections the community relies on and opens up kinds of data that did not exist a few years ago. It brings together three tasks: Data Collection, which secures Dutch participation in the major international surveys; Online Panels, home to the LISS panel and the new PANL platform; and the Netherlands Media Corpus, which turns the country’s scattered media archives into a single resource for research.`,
   `For researchers at ODISSEI member organisations, this means free or low-cost access to some of the best data in Europe. The LISS panel offers a probability-based sample of around 7,500 people in 5,000 Dutch households (to be expanded in the Deepfield project), and every year the LISS ODISSEI calls fund new studies at no cost to the researcher. The SHARE, ESS, EVS and the Dutch Parliamentary Election Study provide a long view of Dutch society and place it alongside the rest of Europe. Through data donation, online experiments and mobile sensing, LISS now captures what people do as well as what they say.`,
   `Behind it all is one goal: data that can be found, linked, and analysed. Observatory data flows into the ODISSEI Portal for discovery and into SANE for secure analysis of sensitive material such as detailed geography or digital trace data. Soon, a researcher will be able to start from a survey answer, follow it to the news article a respondent read, and analyse both in one secure place.`,
   `2027 is the year these pieces start to connect. PANL opens its public beta version, giving researchers a fast, GDPR-compliant way to recruit Dutch-speaking participants and annotators, complementing the probability-based LISS panel. The Web & Media Diets Observatory fields its first full wave in LISS, recording which news and platforms people actually consume and linking that to the archived content itself at the KB National Library (KB) and other heritage institutions. And finally, the international surveys are moving toward cost-efficient, self-administered fieldwork, supported by a shared question bank, new sampling methods, and experiments to reach groups surveys usually miss.`],
  status:{big:"1,912",cap:"known publications based on LISS data",text:[
   `In 2026, the Observatory ran at full strength while laying the foundations for the Macroscope project. The LISS panel recruited a new booster sample aimed at younger people, migrants and single-person households, moved to a mobile-first respondent portal, started building a mobile research app and added multilingual search to the LISS Data Archive. Data donation through Port is now a standard LISS feature; restricted LISS variables can be analysed in SANE. PANL completed its migration to the NEXT platform and entered a controlled soft launch.`,
   `For the international surveys, two Macroscope postdocs started at Utrecht University and Tilburg University; ESS, EVS and WVS tested self-administration in the LISS panel, and a question bank compatible with DCAT (Data Catalogue Vocabulary) and the ODISSEI Portal took shape, using LLMs to map overlap between questionnaires. The Netherlands Media Corpus team was hired at UvA and the KB, the corpus was scoped, and the Web & Media Diets Observatory was piloted in LISS together with the AlgoSoc project.`]},
  plans:[
   {t:"International studies continue",s:"SHARE participation, national ESS coordination, and the next election study.",full:`ODISSEI keeps the Netherlands in SHARE, funds national ESS coordination, and prepares for the Dutch Parliamentary Election Study at the next election.`},
   {key:true,t:"Smarter, cheaper surveys",s:"The first version of a shared question bank from international questionnaires.",full:`The first version of the shared question bank will be delivered, built from ESS, EVS, and other international questionnaires.`},
   {t:"A strong, representative panel",s:"Caring for LISS, new recruitment tests, the Core Study and the annual LISS call.",full:`LISS will continue to care for its panel and test new ways to recruit hard-to-reach groups. The annual LISS Core Study and the ODISSEI LISS panel call for research proposals will continue, and survey and media metadata will be registered in the ODISSEI Portal.`},
   {t:"Reaching under-represented groups",s:"Experiments in the election study or SHARE, and faster access to population samples.",full:`Experiments to better reach under-represented groups are planned for the Dutch Parliamentary Election Study or SHARE, and for the ESS in 2028. Work on faster access to population samples will start.`},
   {t:"Making study design easier",s:"A simple interface to set up surveys and balance cost and quality.",full:`LISS panel will start developing a simple interface that helps researchers set up surveys and experiments and balance cost and quality.`},
   {t:"Secure and FAIR by default",s:"GPS, accelerometer and digital-trace data from LISS become available in SANE.",full:`More sensitive LISS panel data, including GPS, accelerometer and digital-trace data, will become available through SANE.`},
   {key:true,t:"Fast, flexible recruitment with PANL",s:"A public beta for the ODISSEI community, with dedicated PANL calls.",full:`PANL opens its public beta version to the wider ODISSEI community, and ODISSEI will run dedicated PANL calls to support usage. New features include booking and scheduling for offline studies and an environment for longer-term work with groups of annotators.`},
   {key:true,t:"Linking people to the media they consume",s:"The first wave of the Web & Media Diets Observatory, on at least two platforms.",full:`The first wave of the Web & Media Diets Observatory will go live, covering at least two platforms. A new tool will let researchers retrieve archived web pages from the KB web collection by URL, and a proof-of-concept pipeline will ingest the news websites that respondents actually visited.`},
   {t:"Frameworks for media usage research",s:"A metadata model, a position paper and pilot research projects.",full:`A refined metadata model, a position paper on the Netherlands Media Corpus and a plan for audiovisual collections will be delivered. Pilot research projects will test two routes: from individuals to the content they saw, and from content in one archive to related content in others.`}],
  items:[["Data collections: international surveys",773953],["Online panels: LISS and PANL",1882410,"incl. €216,410 PANL"],["Netherlands Media Corpus",518913]],
  budgetText:`The planned 2027 expenditure for the Observatory is €3,175,226. Part of this total budget will be directed to data collections, to support international surveys and innovations, totalling €773,953. The investment in maintenance and improvements of Online Panels amounts to €1,882,410, including €216,410 for the launch and maintenance of PANL. The planned expenditure on the Netherlands Media Corpus is €518,913 for Web and Media diets collection and the first NMC pilots.`},

 {id:"laboratory",name:"Laboratory",color:"var(--ws-lab)",img:"img/odissei-network.jpg",alt:"Researchers talking during a break at the ODISSEI Conference",credit:"Photo: nelinadasha.com for ODISSEI",
  head:`New tools for studying life in a <span class="hl">digital, connected</span> society`,
  aim:"Shared tools for digital traces, experiments and networks",
  lead:`The Laboratory develops shared tools that let researchers study digital traces and social networks responsibly, so research groups can build on common tools instead of creating their own.`,
  facts:[["10","Data donation studies a year, the 2027 target"],["~160","Reuses a year of PLANET-NL's open-source software"]],
  highlights:["D3I becomes a full service","Online experiments open to all researchers","New methods for safer network research"],
  intro:[
   `Much of everyday life now leaves digital traces, from the videos people watch to the messages they send. At the same time, researchers want to understand how people interact and how the networks of family, work, school and neighbourhood ties shape opportunities. The Laboratory develops shared tools that let researchers study these questions responsibly, so that research groups can build on common tools instead of creating their own for each project.`,
   `The Laboratory works on three areas. Data donation lets people voluntarily share the data that platforms such as Google, Instagram or TikTok hold about them, with full control over what they share. Mass online experiments allow thousands of people to take part in the same live, interactive study, for example, a group decision or a game of cooperation. Large network tools, developed by PLANET-NL (formerly POPNET), help researchers analyse networks that connect the entire Dutch population, using CBS data, while protecting people’s privacy.`,
   `In 2026, researchers in the Netherlands gained free access to the data donation software, and data donation studies can now be set up without writing code. The first large online experiment was completed, and PLANET-NL released software that checks whether network data could reveal someone’s identity. In 2027, the focus is on renewing, connecting and opening up these tools: a major upgrade of the data donation software, links between data donation, PANL and a new research app, the first large experiment with LISS households, and new methods for safely analysing population-wide networks. The main risks come from outside: new European rules could limit data donation, and skilled developers are hard to find.`],
  status:{big:"4 of 6",cap:"recent data donation studies used D3I's standard templates",text:[
   `The Laboratory’s tools are moving from research projects to services that researchers can rely on. The D3I data donation project set up a licensing arrangement that lets researchers in the Netherlands use its software for free, is signing agreements with universities, and is building a dedicated support team. Researchers can now prepare data donation studies entirely through easy-to-use screens, and four of six recent studies used D3I’s standard templates. Plans for a new research app were agreed with Centerdata and Utrecht University, and a product owner and community manager started in October 2026.`,
   `For online experiments, the first use case was completed, a second one on group deliberation was designed, and a secure online workspace for running experiments was built at SURF, with a first pilot in the LISS panel planned for late 2026.`,
   `PLANET-NL maintains free, open-source software for network analysis, which is reused around 160 times a year, and has released tools to check and reduce the risk that network data reveal individuals. It launched a City Network Science Lab with the municipality of Amsterdam, and two other national statistics offices are involved in creating population-wide network data.`]},
  plans:[
   {key:true,t:"Data donation as a full service",s:"D3I becomes fully operational and reaches agreements with more universities.",full:`D3I is expected to be fully operational as a service and to reach agreements with more universities.`},
   {t:"A major upgrade for data donation",s:"Photos and videos, on-device processing, and a personal report for participants.",full:`New features will let participants share data more easily, keep some processing on their own device, donate photos and videos, and download a personal report about their own data.`},
   {t:"Connected tools",s:"Data donation, PANL and the new research app are linked.",full:`Data donation, PANL and the new research app will be linked so participants can move smoothly between them.`},
   {t:"A research app in practice",s:"Two new data collection modules, developed and tested with Centerdata.",full:`Two new data collection modules will be developed and tested with Centerdata, and the app will be promoted to researchers.`},
   {t:"Better visibility",s:"Tools listed in the Portal and European catalogues; target of 10 studies a year.",full:`Data collection tools will be listed in the ODISSEI Portal and European catalogues, and an outreach campaign will include visits to institutions. Target: 10 data donation studies a year using ODISSEI’s licences.`},
   {key:true,t:"Online experiments open to all researchers",s:"The workspace opens early in the year; a first large experiment with LISS households.",when:"Early 2027",full:`The online experiment workspace will become widely available early in 2027, with a support plan. A first large experiment will run with LISS households, a pilot will follow on PANL once it opens, and a call for experiments in the LISS panel will be published.`},
   {t:"Ready-made building blocks",s:"A first library of reusable experiment components.",full:`A first library of reusable experiment components will be released, and the team will explore AI assistants that help researchers build experiments.`},
   {key:true,t:"Safer network research",s:"Better protection for network data, a map-based explorer and faster community detection.",full:`PLANET-NL will develop methods that protect network data as well as ordinary tables, a visual tool for exploring networks on a map, and faster ways to find communities within very large networks.`},
   {t:"From research to policy",s:"The City Network Science Lab grows into a broader policy lab.",full:`The City Network Science Lab will grow into a broader policy lab, and community events will bring together researchers, policymakers and statistics offices.`}],
  items:[["Data donation (D3I)",697597],["Mass online experiments",176146],["Large network tools (PLANET-NL)",620762]],
  budgetText:`Planned 2027 expenditure is €1,494,504. Of this total budget, €697,597 will be allocated to upgrading the Data Donation facilities, ensuring the delivery of the D3I platform as a full service. Investments in mass online experiments will amount to €176,146, ensuring that the online experiments platform will be operational and ready for use. Finally, a budget of €620,762 will be allocated to securely leverage large network tools.`},

 {id:"hub",name:"Hub",color:"var(--ws-hub)",img:"img/hub.jpg",alt:"Two researchers working together at a table",credit:"Photo: ODISSEI",
  head:`The people behind the <span class="hl">infrastructure</span>`,
  aim:"Support, training and coordination",
  lead:`Data and tools matter only when researchers know they exist, can get help using them, and trust they are used responsibly. The Hub is where researchers find that help.`,
  facts:[["73","Grant applications in 2026: 27 microdata, 46 LISS"],["2 → 3","Social Data Science fellowships a year"]],
  highlights:["Support measured from January","One process for every access grant","An Ethics Forum with a public voice"],
  intro:[
   `Data and tools matter only when researchers know they exist, can get help using them, and trust they are used responsibly. The Hub is where researchers find that help. It answers questions, awards grants that provide access to data and computing, trains new users, offers guidance on ethical and legal questions, and coordinates ODISSEI-run projects.`,
   `The Hub has three parts. Support includes the ODISSEI front desk, access grants, fellowships and advice from the Social Data Science (SoDa) team, the FAIR Expertise Hub, which helps researchers make their data findable and reusable, and an ethics help desk. Education delivers summer schools, workshops, tutorials and training in computational social science. Coordination covers project management, governance, reporting, communication and events for the SSHOC-NL, Macroscope and PDI-SSH projects.`,
   `In 2026, the Hub formalised its work: support procedures were documented, a new system for tracking requests and usage (CRM) went live, and the ethics help desk was extended. Grant calls were popular, with 27 applications for microdata access and 46 for the LISS panel. In 2027, the Hub will measure its support for the first time, introduce one simple process for all access grants, set up an Ethics Forum that includes members of the public, and pilot a new skills curriculum that combines online self-study and in-person training. The main challenge is capacity: as the infrastructure grows, so does the scale of operations and support delivered.`],
  status:{big:"20",cap:"early career researchers trained at the ODISSEI-SICSS Summer School",text:[
   `The Hub became more professional and better organised in 2026. Support procedures for SANE and the grant calls were documented, more than one person now has access to every help desk mailbox, and a new system for logging requests and usage (CRM) went live in August. ODISSEI now has 44 member organisations, including LUMC and the Netherlands Court of Audit, which joined in 2026. The grants continue to attract many submissions.`,
   `The SoDa team continued its fellowships and around 15 consultations a year, and contributed to projects such as NL-LENS, which models life courses using administrative data. The FAIR Expertise Hub published an action plan for better data practices and ODISSEI co-organised the CBS Replication Games, where 18 researchers investigated whether six published studies could be reproduced.`,
   `The ODISSEI-SICSS Summer School trained 20 early career researchers; ODISSEI held its first Early Career Day and a conference marking ten years of ODISSEI, and it organised its first Research Infrastructure Day with CLARIAH. On the coordination side, the 13 joint tasks were set up across the three projects run by the infrastructure, the Macroscope consortium agreement was signed, the task planning process was simplified, and the SSHOC-NL progress report for 2025 was approved by NWO.`]},
  plans:[
   {key:true,t:"Measuring support",s:"All requests and usage recorded from January, plus a short satisfaction survey.",when:"January",full:`From January 2027, all support requests and usage activity will be recorded, so ODISSEI can monitor which facilities each member uses and how quickly researchers get an answer. Users will receive a short satisfaction survey when their grant, fellowship or SANE project ends.`},
   {t:"Clearer information",s:"A guide for data stewards and a page explaining who can use what.",when:"Twice a year",full:`A guide for data stewards at member organisations will be published, with an online introduction twice a year, and a web page will clearly explain who can use SANE, the Portal, and the grants.`},
   {key:true,t:"One simple process for all access grants",s:"One standard procedure with short applications for every grant.",full:`Applying for microdata access, LISS panel time, secure computing or expert support will follow one standard procedure, with short applications. Calls will be designed to reach early career researchers and disciplines that are not yet using the infrastructure much.`},
   {t:"More expert help",s:"SoDa fellowships grow from two to three a year; a FAIR position at the VU.",full:`The SoDa team will increase its fellowships from two to three a year and expand its consultations. The FAIR Expertise Hub will be strengthened with a dedicated position at the VU and will help more data providers join the secure data route.`},
   {key:true,t:"An Ethics Forum with a public voice",s:"A new forum with public representatives, and an easier-to-find help desk.",full:`A new Ethics Forum will include public representatives recruited through an open call. The ethics help desk will become easier to find and will be built into grant and SANE applications.`},
   {t:"Practical guidance on hard questions",s:"Written guidance on AI with sensitive data, consent, and access from outside academia.",full:`Written guidance will cover access by users outside academia, the use of AI with sensitive data, consent for data donation, standard agreements for data providers, and how to link survey participants to CBS and historical records. The data protection impact assessment for Macroscope will be completed.`},
   {t:"Learning by doing",s:"The Summer School, AI workshops, and a new skills curriculum pilot.",full:`The ODISSEI Summer School will continue, along with workshops on topics such as AI in social science research and a second Research Infrastructure Day. The SoDa team will pilot two modules of a new skills curriculum.`},
   {t:"Stronger user voice",s:"The Early Career Board continues; Research Infrastructure Day becomes annual.",full:`The Early Career Board will continue advocating for early career researchers in the community. The Research Infrastructure Day will become a standalone annual event that brings together infrastructure providers and educators.`},
   {t:"Smooth coordination and outreach",s:"Support for governance, reporting to NWO, and all task plans on time.",full:`The Hub will continue to support the governance bodies, reporting to NWO and compliance across partners, with all task plans delivered on time. Communication and outreach activities will remain a core instrument in reaching the community.`}],
  items:[["Coordination and shared reporting",848569],["Education and training",469769],["Support: front desk, SoDa, ethics",1224168]],
  budgetText:`Planned 2027 expenditure is €2,542,505. Of these resources, €848,569 will support cross-infrastructure Coordination and shared reporting activities. €469,769 is directed towards educational activities, such as development and delivery of the training programmes or ODISSEI Summer School. Finally, €1,224,168 will finance a wide array of support activities, ranging from front desk support and SoDa team methodological support to ethical support.`}
];

/* Budget totals as stated in the plan (line items may differ by rounding) */
const STATED={data:3052182,observatory:3175226,laboratory:1494504,hub:2542505};

/* ---------- Message from the Chair ---------- */
const CHAIR={
 name:"Daniel Oberski",
 role:"Scientific Director & Chair of the Management Board",
 img:"img/oberski.jpg",
 quote:`Researchers should be able to concentrate on their scientific questions, with confidence that sensitive data are handled responsibly.`,
 text:[
  `[HOLDING] The plans for 2027 are very exciting and particularly the prospect of bringing different ways of studying society into the same research workflow. Surveys, digital traces, experiments, historical collections and population networks each reveal something different. Connecting them gives us opportunities to ask questions that none of these sources could answer alone.`,
  `The developments around LISS are a compelling example. Integrating data donation, interactive experiments and mobile measurements with a probability-based panel opens up new possibilities for understanding everyday behaviour. Connecting participants’ media use to the content they encounter could help us investigate how information, attitudes and social circumstances interact. This is where collaboration between the social sciences and humanities becomes scientifically productive.`,
  `Of course, more data do not automatically produce better knowledge. We still need to establish what our measures capture, whose experiences are represented, and where uncertainty enters our conclusions. I see an important role for our infrastructure in making that methodological work possible, alongside providing access to new sources and computational tools.`,
  `In 2027, we will also work to make those possibilities more accessible. Further development of SANE and the Data Access Broker will help connect discovery, permission and secure analysis. Researchers should be able to concentrate on their scientific questions, with confidence that sensitive data are handled responsibly.`,
  `Equally encouraging is the investment in people such as research software engineers, methodological support, fellowships and training. Infrastructure becomes useful through the expertise of colleagues who help researchers design studies, solve problems and develop approaches that others can reuse.`,
  `Our ambition is substantial, and delivery will require careful choices and sustained collaboration. I look forward to seeing these connections take shape in research projects, and to working with our partners to build an infrastructure whose value is demonstrated in the quality of the research it enables across our disciplines.`
 ]
};

/* ---------- Why ODISSEI matters ---------- */
const WHY={
 intro:[
  `ODISSEI gives social scientists in the Netherlands shared access to data, secure computing, tools and expertise that individual institutions would otherwise have to organise for themselves. ODISSEI has 44 member organisations. In 2027, much of this work is carried out through three projects, SSHOC-NL, Macroscope and PDI-SSH, which are coordinated as joint SSH-RI task plans with CLARIAH. This plan describes the work through ODISSEI’s four workstreams: the Data Facility, the Observatory, the Laboratory and the Hub.`,
  `Many research questions about Dutch society depend on data that are sensitive, collected over long periods, or gathered in new ways. Examples are questions on inequality, ageing, education, labour markets, health and digital life. Working with such data requires secure environments, legal agreements, long-term data collection and specialised methods. ODISSEI provides these as shared services, so that researchers at member organisations can use them for their own projects.`],
 closing:`The three projects currently run by ODISSEI have complementary roles: SSHOC-NL builds foundations, Macroscope extends what researchers can do with them, and PDI-SSH helps make the resulting services sustainable. Together they aim for better infrastructure, which supports better research and, through it, a better society.`
};
/* [workstream, title, short summary, full text] */
const CAPS=[
 ["Data Facility","Secure access to sensitive data","CBS microdata, SANE and the OSSC, so institutions do not each build their own.",`CBS microdata, linked administrative records and digital trace data can only be analysed in protected environments. ODISSEI supports access to CBS microdata and offers SANE (Secure Analysis Environment) and the OSSC (ODISSEI Secure Supercomputer) for sensitive data from other providers, so that institutions do not each need to build their own. Data providers keep control over their data, and researchers follow one brokered process. The number of projects on SANE grew from 20 in mid-2025 to 67 in June 2026.`],
 ["Data Facility","Finding and reusing data","The Portal, the knowledge graph and FAIR archiving at DANS.",`The ODISSEI Portal, the knowledge graph and FAIR archiving at DANS help researchers find datasets, connect them, and keep results available for future research. The Portal holds metadata from 19 of ODISSEI’s 27 scientific members and attracts more than 500 unique visitors a month.`],
 ["Observatory","Data collections that follow society over time","LISS, SHARE, ESS, EVS, WVS and GGP, plus PANL for fast recruitment.",`Questions about social mobility, ageing, inequality and family formation need data collected consistently over many years. ODISSEI supports collections that follow the same individuals, households and families over time, and collections that repeat the same questions with new samples to track trends. These include the LISS panel, which is representative of the Dutch population, and the Dutch participation in SHARE (Survey of Health, Ageing and Retirement in Europe), ESS (European Social Survey), EVS (European Values Study), WVS (World Values Survey) and GGP (Gender and Generation Programme), which allows comparison with other countries. PANL adds a flexible platform for fast participant recruitment, and historical records from the Historical Sample of the Netherlands extend the view across generations. The LISS Data Archive has more than 14,000 registered users, and 1,912 publications based on LISS data are known.`],
 ["Laboratory","Tools for new types of data","Data donation, mass online experiments and the Netherlands Media Corpus.",`Researchers increasingly need digital traces, behavioural data and experiments alongside surveys. Through data donation, mass online experiments and the Netherlands Media Corpus, ODISSEI offers shared tools for collecting digital trace data, running large interactive experiments and connecting survey answers with media content. These support research on topics such as social media, misinformation and online behaviour, without each project having to build its own tools.`],
 ["Laboratory","Methods for population-scale analysis","PLANET-NL software for networks that cover the Dutch population.",`PLANET-NL provides open-source software for analysing networks that cover the Dutch population and for assessing the risk that such data reveal individuals. This allows these methods to be used safely within secure environments.`],
 ["Hub","Support, training and coordination","Grants, fellowships, ethics guidance and the Summer School.",`Infrastructure is only useful when researchers can use it with confidence. The Hub offers access grants, fellowships, consultations, ethical and legal guidance, and training such as the ODISSEI-SICSS Summer School. It also coordinates ODISSEI’s work with CLARIAH, SURF, DANS, CBS and European partners.`]
];

